  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nNear term through Monday: Finally are seeing a few showers over\nnorthern WI this morning. Light rain for sure, with top winner \nthus far not even up to 0.10 inch in Vilas County. No lightning \nas instability is minimal. Most areas south of highway 8 will \nstay dry this morning with stronger shortwave forcing crossing \nUpper Michigan/Lake Superior. Mix of low clouds and some fog could\noccur this morning as weak warm front lifts through, just before \nfront entering nw WI shifts through. Still looks like a brief \nshower (15-30% chance) may occur early this afternoon over mainly \nDoor County. CAMS point to northern Door, including Washington \nIsland, as most favored area for any rain. Despite the passage of \nthe front, which is essentially a glorified wind shift from S to \nNW, highs will reach the lower to middle 80s this afternoon. NW \nwinds may gust to 20 mph with the mixing behind the front. \n\nSteady NW flow and dry weather persists into Monday as we will be\nwithin influence of high pressure over the plains. Primary \ntakeaway for Monday is it looks like a less humid day across the \nboard. Temps will be similar to today. \n\nMinimal rain chances rest of the week: As early as Monday night\nshortwaves in nw flow and approaching front will bring next chance\nfor scattered showers and maybe a storm late evening and overnight\nespecially central WI. This front sweeps through fairly quickly on\nTuesday and a result, chances for showers and storms that looked\ndecent a couple days ago have faded fast, down to a 20-30% chance\nduring the afternoon into the evening central to east-central WI.\nEven so, given daytime instability (CAPEs over 1000J/kg) and shear\nover 40 kts, cannot completely rule out a stronger storm. Better\nchances for severe and heavy rain, by far, now look to stay southwest\nand south of the area. Recently, it appeared that some rain may\nlinger into Wednesday, but with quicker fropa, it now looks dry\nfor Wednesday. Another chance for showers and some storms shows up\non Friday with sharpening nw flow bringing shortwave/front through.\nBetter instability though looks to remain southwest of the state.\n\nAs of right now, no widespread rain chances are in the offing\ngiven this kind of splitting pattern we are stuck in, with \nstronger northern stream forcing staying north and greatest \ninstability gradient persisting south. That is too bad as we could\ncertainly use some rain. Rainfall deficits since mid July are now\nrunning over 3 inches for much of north-central WI, and 1 to 2 \ninches elsewhere that have not been fortunate to see isolated\nsummer drenching storms. Thus, drought conditions have expanded\nsteadily. As of early July not even 50% of our forecast area was \nin a D0 (Abnormally Dry) classification. Now, as of the latest\ndrought monitor (8/11), over 90% of our area is within D1 (Moderate\nDrought), with parts of Oneida and Vilas counties into D2 (Severe\nDrought).",
